course: everyday
lesson: 417
title: Just one more game
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At half past five Kai said «one more game». At six he said «one more game, the last one». At half six: «this one really is the last». At seven: «one more, I was so close». At half seven his little sister wrote «ONE MORE GAME» on a sticky note and stuck it on his forehead. By then his fish fingers were stone cold and two hours had gone. Kai honestly felt every single game was the last one. «One more» is a promise that is very easy to break, because it is made again every time.
explain: Leo tried a cleverer excuse. His mum called him for dinner and he shouted: «I can't pause, it's online!» That was true: you cannot pause a Dota 2 match, and leaving lets the whole team down. But his mum asked the question that ended the argument: «Then why did you start a forty-minute match ten minutes before dinner?» Leo had no answer. Now he checks the clock before he presses «find match», not after.
explain: Some games are built to keep you playing. Ruby noticed that the end screen of her game had a huge, bright, bouncing «PLAY AGAIN?» button and a tiny grey «quit» in the corner. Jayden's game said each level took «just five minutes», and he played twelve of them in one hour. Ollie's friends kept messaging «come on, one more» when he had to go, and he felt torn between staying with them and his mum calling him. He typed «gotta go, tomorrow?» and they all played the next day, as if nothing had happened, because real friends wait.
explain: The children found their own tricks. Priya put a kitchen timer shaped like a chicken next to her console; when it squawked, she stopped, because it is easier to obey a beep than to argue with yourself. Zara kept a game diary and was shocked: eleven hours of games that week and forty minutes of reading. Noah's sister once unplugged the TV in the middle of his Tekken match; he was furious, their mum said unplugging is not the way, and now the family gives a five-minute warning. And Mia, in the middle of a huge Minecraft castle, wrote a note, «next: the drawbridge and the moat», and switched off calmly, because she knew exactly where to start tomorrow.
fact: «Just one more game» is easy to say again and again, so the games add up to hours without you noticing.
fact: Deciding how many games or how long before you start is much easier than deciding while you play, when the fun pulls you to go on.
fact: Online matches usually cannot be paused, and leaving early lets your team down.
fact: Because online games cannot be paused, it is wise to start them only when there is enough time to finish.
fact: Many games are designed with big bright buttons, rewards and short levels to keep people playing longer.
fact: Many short levels can add up to a long time.
fact: A timer or an alarm can be the one that says «stop», so you do not have to argue with yourself.
fact: Writing down how long you play shows the real picture, which is often more than you guessed.
fact: A warning a few minutes before the end makes stopping easier than a sudden switch-off.
fact: Leaving yourself a note of what to do next makes it easier to stop a building game and start again later.
fact: Good friends understand when you have to go and will play again another day.
exercise: Why did Kai play for two hours when he only meant to play one more game? | He said «one more» again after every game | His game had no way to stop at all | His sister kept starting new games for him | The games were much longer than usual
exercise: How did Kai feel about each game while he played? | That every one would be the last | That he was wasting his evening | That his sister was right all along | That his dinner was getting cold
exercise: What is the best time to decide how many games to play? | Before you start the first one | In the middle of a really good game | When you have just lost a game | When a grown-up calls you for dinner
exercise: Why could Leo not pause his match when dinner was ready? | It was an online match with other players | His controller had no pause button on it at all | His mum had locked the pause menu | The game was in a special story mode
exercise: What was Leo's real mistake? | Starting a long match just before dinner | Playing a game that his mum didn't like at all | Choosing the wrong hero for his team | Playing online with players he knew
exercise: What does Leo do now before pressing «find match»? | Checks whether there is time to finish | Asks his team to play more slowly | Turns off the sound so he can't hear | Hides the clock so it doesn't worry him
exercise: Why was Ruby's «play again» button so big and bright? | To pull players into playing more | Because Ruby's screen was too dark | So that small children can read it | Because the quit button was broken
exercise: What did Jayden discover about the «five-minute» levels? | Twelve of them took a whole hour | Each one really took only one minute | The levels got shorter as he went on | They took exactly five minutes in total
exercise: How did Ollie feel when his friends said «come on, one more»? | Torn between his friends and his mum | Glad to have a reason to stay longer | Cross that his friends were so rude | Bored of the game and ready to leave
exercise: What happened after Ollie said «gotta go, tomorrow?» | They all played again the next day | His friends never asked him again | His friends left him out of the squad | His mum let him stay for one more
exercise: Why did Priya's chicken timer work? | It is easier to obey a beep than yourself | The squawk switched the console off | Her friends could hear it on voice chat and teased her | The timer took away her controller
exercise: What did Zara's game diary show her? | She played far more than she read | She played less than she thought | She only played at the weekend | She read more than she played
exercise: Why was unplugging the TV not a good way to stop Noah? | A sudden stop makes people furious | It can break the television at once | It is not allowed by the game rules | It only works on very old consoles
exercise: What did Noah's family agree instead of unplugging? | A five-minute warning before the end | A rule that the TV stays unplugged | That his sister plays every other game | That Noah only plays when they are out
exercise: Why could Mia stop calmly in the middle of her castle? | She wrote a note of what to do next | Her castle was already completely finished | Her dad turned the game off for her | She had saved a copy of a friend's castle
exercise: Which of these needs a bit of courage? | Saying «I have to go» when friends beg you to stay | Staying on because your friends ask you to | Starting a new match without telling anyone | Pressing «play again» before the timer ends
test: Mum: «dinner's on the table!» Kai: «one more, I swear it's the last». It's his fifth «last». What does the lesson say? | «One more» is a promise that's easy to break | Fifth time is when it's really the last | Mum should wait until he wins one | It's fine if he swears it's the last
test: «can't pause mum it's ONLINE, I'll get reported». It's true, but what should Leo have done? | Not started a long match right before dinner | Played the match with the sound off | Told his team to pause the whole match for him | Quit halfway and left his team alone
test: Ollie's squad: «noooo stay, one more, don't be lame». Best reply? | «gotta go, same time tomorrow?» | «ok fine, just one, don't tell my mum» | «you're all losers anyway, bye» | «I'll just stay muted and play»
test: Jayden: «each level's like five mins, it's nothing». He plays 12. Roughly how long is that? | About an hour | About five minutes | About twelve minutes | About half an hour
test: Ruby: «why's the play again button massive and the quit one tiny??» Which answer is true? | It's made to keep you playing | Quit buttons are always hard to make | It's an accident nobody noticed | Big buttons are only for small kids
test: Sister yanks the plug mid-Tekken round. Noah: «I'm gonna kill you!!» Mum: «Nobody's killing anyone.» What's the fairer plan? | A five-minute warning before stopping | Noah unplugs her things in return | No more warnings, just switch-off | Noah gets to play two hours extra
test: Priya: «a chicken timer is so babyish lol». What did she find out? | It helped her stop without arguing with herself | It made her play much longer than before, not shorter | Her friends laughed and she threw it away | It only works for younger children
test: Zara guesses she plays «like, 3 hours a week». The diary says 11. What's the lesson? | Writing time down shows the real picture | Guesses are usually too high, not too low | Diaries always add on extra hours | Three hours and eleven are about the same
test: Mia, half a castle built, mum says bedtime. What's the smart move? | Write what's next and switch off | Keep going until the castle is finished | Leave it running so it saves itself | Ask a friend to finish it overnight
test: Teacher: «Why is it harder to stop in the middle than to plan before?» Best answer? | While playing, the fun pulls you to go on | It is easier to stop when you are losing | Plans made before always get forgotten | Games stop on their own if you plan
test: Mate: «pfft, real gamers don't stop for dinner». What's true? | Stopping when you need to is normal | Real gamers skip meals to stay on | Only beginners stop for dinner | Dinner can be eaten during the match
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a repeated undertaking to play «one more game» commonly fails to be honoured. | The undertaking is renewed upon the conclusion of every game | The games are of a duration exceeding one hour each | Parents customarily forbid the final game | Consoles are incapable of being switched off
exam: It is given that an online match cannot be suspended and that a meal is to be served in ten minutes. Select the course of conduct consistent with the established facts. | To refrain from commencing the match until after the meal | To commence the match and abandon it when called | To commence the match and request that the meal be delayed | To commence the match with the sound disabled
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a large, brightly coloured «play again» control adjacent to a small «quit» control? | It is a design intended to prolong the period of play | It is a provision for the visually impaired exclusively | It is an error in the manufacture of the game | It is a reward conferred upon skilled players
exam: Determine which of the following measures is most likely to facilitate the cessation of play without dispute. | A warning issued some minutes before the agreed end | The sudden disconnection of the electricity supply | The removal of all clocks from the room | The promise of an additional game on each occasion
